WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.960
Illustrative: Bob's replies are representative, checker
output is real, and grey bars cover run values.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.801
A customer asks what it will take to modernise
their legacy app, and what it will cost.

3
00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:12.902
Four Bob commands turn their code
into the documents an SI prices from.

4
00:00:12.902 --> 00:00:15.960
We'll follow one bug from the code
all the way to a line in the price.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.072
Each command runs in its own Bob mode, and
each mode plays one role on an SI team.

6
00:00:22.072 --> 00:00:24.960
After every command, you run a checker.

7
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:33.167
First, set up once: open the kit in IBM
Bob, trust the folder, and check Python.

8
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.130
Discover. You choose a run name,
acme-1 here, and Bob records evidence:

9
00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:13.370
a file and line for what it read, or
a named gap for what it couldn't.

10
00:02:36.241 --> 00:02:45.600
Assess. Bob turns the evidence into findings, an
assessment report and one annex per migration target.

11
00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:44.330
Plan. Bob orders the work into packages
and staffs them from the roster.

12
00:04:39.395 --> 00:04:48.312
Price. Bob writes effort rows and a draft
statement of work. Your own tools do the money.

13
00:06:13.886 --> 00:06:15.550
That's the draft pack.

14
00:06:32.087 --> 00:06:36.080
One line of code, traced to one line of the price.

15
00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:42.547
When the customer asks why the SOW includes
this fix, you can show them the line.

16
00:06:42.587 --> 00:06:51.560
To try it yourself: open the kit, trust the folder, pick
SI Architect, and type slash discover with your run name.
